95554936191261253'}\\\",13.485803079903544508955948061355,21.188019497716894977168949771689,5.5107000000000000000000000000000,14.9999904000,959232,8394032,39.021910636789916051650424751653,-407.33274707624449351121900851876,726.59764016866023089053806933168,,,\\\"{'0.12': None, '0.15': None, '0.18': None}\\\",\\\"NS: pre-entry/intraperiod/terminal cash breach, no ordinary return\\\",\\\"{'terminal_normalized_PAT': '119.17301812500000000000000000000', 'profit_growth_factor': '10.479627864798021434460016488046', 'share_count_factor': '0.88572452428106063927323603245735', 'implied_terminal_PE': None, 'current_normalized_PE': '13.631488211046990931574608408904', 'multiple_factor': None, 'product_price_factor': None, 'note': 'Accounting identity for surviving conditional paths, not independently assumed P/E. Implied terminal P/E includes accumulated cash less debt/reserve. No dividends to post-record-date entrant.'}\\\"\\r\\n\"","\"title\"","\"document_date\"","\"retrieved\"","\"sections_reviewed\"","\"source_type\"","\"copyright_policy\"","\"FY2025–26 annual report\"","\"https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31280_RAPIDFLEET_2025_2026_A_19045203_03092026184433.pdf\"","\"2026-10-23\"","19045203","\"8bb7aa667ed9d6b193a89f9934e69e92db8293130de5768d7bf8cfede8c8372d\"","\"PDF123–134 complete financial audit/CARO/IFC;135–162 accounts and notes;106–113 complete secretarial report/annexure;41–72 AGM notice;76–103 directors and MD&A\"","\"primary issuer or exchange filing\"","\"Authored facts and calculations only, no source document or text dump bundled\"","\"FY2024–25 annual report\"","\"https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_28974_RAPIDFLEET_2024_2025_A_6055495_08092025213555.pdf\"","\"2025-09-08\"","6055495","\"d5f7daba4cf08fa4e0130813d7b3e147db64a26e2664212a76c415d9a500f780\"","\"PDF90–104 complete financial audit/CARO/IFC;105–135 accounts/notes and original RPT; directors report and IPO narrative\"","\"AR24\"","\"Original FY2023–24 annual report\"","\"https://rapidfleet.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Annual-Report_23-24_compressed-1.pdf\"","\"2024-08-08\"","8441211","\"857cb91322a5cddd2384b582e66e98cac839f817dd99fd837ca21b366a23a472\"","\"PDF9–18 complete statutory audit/IFC/CARO;19–40 statements/notes. Audit trail, internal audit and CSR adverse observations explicitly retained.\"","\"Original FY2022–23 annual report and filed accounts\"","\"https://rapidfleet.in/wp-content/uploads/pdfs/AnnualReport/Annual%20Report_22-23-min.pdf\"","\"2023-09-30\"","3945161","\"7ae40a0184695a884b165a77a4d37cb247c9801042cce496b3deda6d36398f9b\"","\"PDF29–38 complete financial audit/IFC/CARO; financials and notes, original-versus-restated bridge\"","\"Original FY2021–22 annual report\"","\"https://rapidfleet.in/wp-content/uploads/pdfs/AnnualReport/Annual%20Report_21-22-min.pdf\"","\"2022-09-05\"","8094875","\"b91d81b3cd1bf90e5490d19cf606ed7a2575c76f1e02570021d5c32e2851c2f9\"","\"PDF9–21 complete statutory audit/CARO/IFC;22–35 financial statements and notes;restatement differences preserved.\"","\"Original FY2020–21 annual report\"","\"https://rapidfleet.in/wp-content/uploads/pdfs/AnnualReport/Annual%20Report_20-21-min.pdf\"","\"2021-11-30\"","7601113","\"a7a29b214db29cb821c6bc6806d8b44a94f668a04a804fca8120298983908af2\"","\"PDF10–19 complete statutory audit/CARO/IFC;20–32 financials and notes. Revenue/DA/capex visually checked;current debt in OCL separately identified.\"","\"Final IPO prospectus\"","\"https://rapidfleet.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Rapid-Fleet_Prospectu.pdf\"","\"2025-03-26\"","3752724","\"280c8eea05e466f036967cb1564d1a8c8993b67c3ef6f49db1dcc98697871650\"","\"PDF risk factors25–50;IPOobjects and sharecapital;business123–146;restatementaudit211–214;financials215–237;litigation. Restatement report not treated as original annual audit.\"","\"FY26 performance and fleet update\"","\"https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPIDFLEET_22052026093226_pr220526__1___1_.pdf\"","\"2026-05-22\"","1167313","\"7c535abe5c0e6c32bf508d9c2d4405efa3bad27ae2ff0a668a3984449dbd45ee\"","\"All5pages;management measures distinguished from audited operating EBITDA. Company August investor-corner copy byte-identical, not a new update.\"","\"Q1FY27 business update\"","\"https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPIDFLEET_31082026085830_business_merged.pdf\"","3405419","\"38ed163426f55e8fc0b1c2c0aa63d53478f36319e0b0008263d19250dec209f3\"","\"All6pages;Q1revenue53.60vs32.93, capex12.35, bank financing amount ND, vehicle and orders;not audited quarter P&L or balance sheet\"","\"Promoter dividend waiver\"","\"https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPIDFLEET_23092026194401_RFMSLBoardMeetingOutcome23092026Signed.pdf\"","2163956","\"7a007f658a566cf02005c36b26434bada5cd88f07159014e7f43fd94aa5734a4\"","\"Both pages: irrevocable FY26 promoter waiver;public entitlement unchanged;18Sep record date\"","\"AGM\"","\"AGM proceedings\"","\"https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPIDFLEET_25092026204100_RFMSLAGMProceedings25092026FinalSigned.pdf\"","10022034","\"10655b6ed70b2d19c0530c6a2ad939d9e3789156caa1f60683cf58f7e8303824\"","\"All5pages;agenda/waiver, no speaker requests;voting confirmation separately sourced\"","\"AGM voting and scrutinizer report\"","\"https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPIDFLEET_28092026175031_SCRUTINIZERR.pdf\"","11840394","\"bcb750b893235b8206db0f52c12c1b16b39f12c0ed9a7f7813a4fe7ee8c10e61\"","\"All8 resolutions;RPTpromoter5,021,500 abstentions;prospectiveFY27 caps passed\"","\"PEER_TCI\"","\"Transport Corporation of India FY25 annual report\"","\"https://tcil.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Annual-Report-2024-25.pdf\"","\"FY2025, available before cutoff\"","23887328","\"ece36d845f0b4a2077d93e08b62d14a3525dde449fa71a60b575336cfeb64728\"","\"Business model and segment scope only, not a full peer financial audit\"","\"Primary issuer annual report\"","\"PEER_VRL\"","\"VRL Logistics FY25 annual report\"","\"https://www.vrlgroup.in/investor_download/Annual_Report_2024_25.pdf\"","6183635","\"8dad6018bbc6aac718b471e024d7612112c6f99a0bffc5611a972f286c8e31fe\"","\"retrieval_note\"","\"supporting_primary_url\"","\"PEER_RITCO\"","\"Ritco Logistics FY25 annual report\"","\"https://www.ritcologistics.com/frontassets/images/Annual_Report_2024-2025.pdf\"","\"Issuer presentation business scope: bulk/FTL, owned and aggregated vehicles, warehousing; no contemporary fleet count or peer multiple inferred. FY25 annual report indexed scope cross-check only.\"","\"HTTP Error 406: Not Acceptable\"","\"https://ritcologistics.com/frontassets/images/Ritco_Presentation.pdf\"","\"# Rapid Fleet Management Services Limited\\n## 1. Executive verdict\\n**Avoid — governance; separately Hold — unresolved data. Exclude from investable rankings.** Rapid Fleet has genuinely expanded its road-transport business and is developing specialized over-dimensional cargo capabilities. But gross sales growth is not yet a clean measure of underlying demand, earnings have converted poorly into cash recently, and governance concerns recur across several audit years. This is a mixed-quality growth/special-situation thesis, not an evidence-backed compounder. A low headline P/E does not compensate automatically for uncertain entry liquidity and disclosure reconciliation.\\n\\nThe decisive facts are ₹60.13 crore receivables, including ₹17.0777 crore unbilled, ₹25.3476 crore FY26 cash capex, and another ₹12.35 crore Q1FY27 capex partly funded by bank finance whose amount and terms are undisclosed. The complete model preserves pre-entry, intramonth and terminal funding failures. A subsequently positive cash balance never repairs an earlier failed return path. [E02–E10]\\n\\n## 2. Research header, gates and data confidence\\n- Exact issuer: Rapid Fleet Management Services Limited, NSE Emerge RAPIDFLEET, ISIN INE0QX901013. It is not Rapid Multimodal Logistics. Incorporated 26 December 2017; public conversion in August 2023. Group/business-origin narratives are not issuer incorporation dates.\\n- Research cutoff: 20 October 2026. Price date: 19 October 2026. Five-year investment clock: 19 October 2026 to 19 October 2031. Later retrieval does not make later information admissible.\\n- Standalone Indian GAAP, Accounting Standards (AS), including AS9, not Ind AS. No subsidiaries, associates or joint ventures in the reviewed scope. One directors-report reference to Ind AS24 does not change the audited accounting basis.\\n- INR crore unless specified. Six fiscal observations FY21–FY26; original annual audit reports and their annexures read, with FY22–24 restated financial series separately retained. FY21 original, FY22–24 IPO restatement, FY25 original and FY26 current vintages are labelled, not silently spliced into a claim of identical restatement scope.\\n- **G1 Conditional:** repeated stewardship/control deficiencies; no demonstrated promoter diversion or severe fraud allegation. GOV3 activates the framework cap and the Avoid — governance classification, independently of whether Gate1 is Fail.\\n- **G2 Hold:** incorrect RPT comparative labels, cash-flow vintage/classification differences, and unverified October cash/new debt materially limit the investment conclusion.\\n- **G3 Conditional:** audited March leverage is manageable, but required financing/collections are not established at entry. Conditional model failure is not proof of actual default.\\n- **G4 Conditional:** 600-share lot and meaningful exit friction. No suspension asserted; current spread and ASM/GSM status remain ND.\\n\\nConfidence is high for audited headline FY25–26 accounts and the dated market tape, medium for historical normalized economics, low for current liquidity, organic volume growth and unconditional return valuation. NS means not scoreable; ND means not disclosed/established; neither means zero.\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and calculation audit\\n| Composite | Known sum | Coverage | Raw | Raw bound | Cap | Final / capped bound |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | 3.18 | 80.00% | NS | ['3.38', '5.18'] | 3 | NS / ['3.00', '3.00'] |\\n| GQS | 2.85 | 60.00% | NS | ['3.25', '6.85'] | 5 | NS / ['3.25', '5.00'] |\\n| IAS | 1.70 | 40.00% | NS | ['2.80', '5.95'] | 3 | NS / ['2.80', '3.00'] |\\n\\nCanonical factors are assigned once and reused. Weights: BQS GOV20%, MOAT20%, REV12%, DUR10%, CAP12%, CASH10%, BAL8%, EXEC8%; GQS REV40%, DUR20%, EARN15%, CAP15%, CASH10%; IAS BQS25%, R35%, D20%, P10%, L10%. Known contributions and every missing weight are preserved. Bound = known sum + missing weight × [1,10], with no renormalization. Decimal precision32 and half-up one-decimal display are used. IAS additionally propagates the already-capped BQS bounds rather than treating its NS input as an unconstrained1–10. Bounds are mathematical limits, not estimated scores. Even a collapsed capped bound does not turn NS into a point score.\\n\\nGOV3 caps BQS and IAS at3; persistent CASH3 caps GQS at5. G1 and G3 are not mechanically marked Fail. GOV±1 and CAP±1 sensitivities, including the discontinuous disappearance of the governance cap at GOV4, are in the calculation audit. The return hurdle is nominal15% annually;12% and18% sensitivities are explicit. Conditional model IRRs are not substituted for R.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence | Basis / contrary evidence |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 3 | Medium | E02, E03, E12, E13 | Recurring control and authorization failures: FY24 no commensurate internal audit, FY24–25 incomplete audit trail, FY25 employee misappropriation, FY26 material RPT approval and MD pay-limit breaches, plus mislabelled RPT comparatives. No promoter diversion alleged. Contrary: Financial and IFC opinions are unmodified. Promoter dividend waiver and subsequent prospective RPT approvals are positives, but do not erase historical lapses. |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium | E04, E05 | Bidding-based road transport and hired capacity are replicable. High historical customer concentration limits bargaining power. Contrary: Specialized ODC execution and customer relationships are useful, but excess returns and switching costs are unverified. |\\n| REV | NS | Low | E06, E04 | The preselected five-year organic window and three-year fallback cannot be isolated from fuel pass-through and owned/hired mix. Nominal reported CAGRs remain diagnostics; underlying REV is NS. Contrary: Reported sales and value added expanded materially. No acquisition was identified, but a clean demand bridge is missing. |\\n| DUR | 6 | Medium | E11, E05 | Existing auto, renewable and project-cargo customers provide accessible demand. The fleet investment and short-tenor orders demonstrate real commercial activity. Contrary: Order cancellations, remaining backlog and current customer concentration are ND; capex alone does not prove future volumes. |\\n| CAP | 4 | Medium | E10, E07 | The recent three-year incremental return proxy is below the analytical13–17% cost-of-capital range. Fleet and receivable investment absorbed substantial capital. Contrary: The five-year proxy is stronger and new fleet cohorts are immature; aggregate returns are not project IRRs. |\\n| CASH | 3 | High | E07, E08 | Recent three-year CFO/PAT is weak; aged and unbilled receivables increased. FY25 printed CFO retains investment gains. Contrary: FY22–23 conversion was stronger and FY26 CFO was positive, although supplier credit materially supported FY22. |\\n| BAL | NS | Low | E09, E14 | October cash/debt and the amount and terms of Q1 bank finance are unknown. Linked scenarios cannot establish actual funding resilience, so BAL is NS. Contrary: March leverage was modest, cash equivalents existed, and audits report no loan default. Gate3 is not automatically Fail. |\\n| EXEC | 5 | Medium | E11, E04 | Reported trip activity, fleet additions and complex ODC delivery demonstrate operating execution. No formal earnings target is invented. Contrary: Fleet rollforward, customer cohorts and collection outcomes remain unreconciled; operational progress is not cash-conversion excellence. |\\n| EARN | 5 | Medium | E06, E08, E01 | FY26 operating profit and normalized current-share earnings improved. FY25 investment gains and IPO weighted-share EPS complicate comparisons. Contrary: Operating EBITDA increased and the other-income contribution fell; earnings growth is not solely non-operating. |\\n| R | NS | Low | E09, E07 | No defensible unconditional five-year IRR exists while entry funding is unknown. Conditional rescue outcomes are not substituted into R. Contrary: The model retains feasible and failed conditional returns and explicit12/15/18% hurdles. |\\n| D | 4 | Medium | E05, E07, E09 | Customer concentration, unbilled/aged receivables and upfront hire funding expose permanent capital loss. A rescued bear case may still dilute and lose value. Contrary: Tangible fleet and March liquidity provide some protection; actual insolvency is not established. |\\n| P | 5 | Medium | E06, E09 | Audited FY26 P/E is about12.4x and does not plainly require exceptional growth. Removing other income raises the earnings multiple. Contrary: March cash is not current excess cash; an inexpensive headline multiple cannot resolve funding or governance risk. |\\n| L | 4 | High | E15 | The prescribed7.504-session reference exit starts atL5. A disclosed one-point implementation haircut reflects15 absent issuer rows and600-share lot granularity. Contrary: All latest20 sessions contain trades and median turnover is similar; the stock is not labelled untradeable. |\\n\\n## 4. Business overview and economic engine\\nRapid Fleet provides full/partial truckload, EXIM inland transportation and specialized project cargo. Its economic model combines owned trucks, attached vehicles and market-hired capacity. It is neither a pure software marketplace nor a wholly owned-fleet operator. In FY26 hire charges were ₹126.6049 crore, about62% of sales; direct freight, handling and other operating expenses add further cost. Hire charges are purchased transport services, not automatically finance leases. Offices/yards are rented; AS accounting does not justify importing an invented Ind AS lease liability. [E04]\\n\\nThe prospectus describes paying80% of hired-trip value before dispatch, with proof-of-delivery and billing preceding customer collection. Consequently, a profitable trip can consume cash well before it creates cash. Owned vehicles add maintenance, insurance, driver costs, replacement and secured truck amortization. Detailed owned/hired sales, EBITDA, utilization, asset ages, disposal values and maintenance-versus-growth capex are ND. A blended EBITDA margin cannot establish either fleet model's unit profitability.\\n\\nFleet observations:226 owned at the March2025 prospectus;250+ in the FY26 update; over300 owned claimed at Q1FY27, with25 additions and ₹12.35 crore capex. These are not an exact opening + additions − disposals reconciliation. Bodies, trailers, cranes and reefers complicate unit definitions. The model does not pretend that a250+ to300+ difference equals an audited50-truck purchase.\\n\\n## 5. Moat, industry and competitive position\\nUseful capabilities include route planning, multi-state permits, safe heavy-cargo execution and repeat corporate relationships. These can win difficult assignments. But road freight is fragmented and tender/rate driven; third-party trucks can be sourced by competitors, and customers can rebid routes. Fuel escalation is partial and contract specific. FY24 top five customers were90.40% of revenue and top ten97.70%; H1FY25 top five84.14% and top ten96.59%. Tamil Nadu supplied58.27% of FY24 revenue. Current FY26 customer concentration is not established. [E05]\\n\\nThe prospectus identifies a third-party technology provider, while later marketing describes a proprietary TMS. Without ownership, cost advantage or retention evidence, software is an operating tool, not a scored intellectual-property moat. Large logistics peers can offer integrated networks; small road carriers offer price competition. No unsourced current peer valuation or broad national TAM is used to justify a premium.\\n\\nBusiness-comparability peer set, not a multiple-transfer screen:\\n- [Ritco Logistics, issuer presentation](https://ritcologistics.com/frontassets/images/Ritco_Presentation.pdf): bulk/FTL logistics with owned and aggregated capacity is economically relevant; warehousing and its broader aggregation platform limit direct margin comparison.\\n- [VRL Logistics, FY25 report](https://www.vrlgroup.in/investor_download/Annual_Report_2024_25.pdf): owned-fleet road transportation informs replacement economics, but its large LTL hub-and-spoke parcel network differs from concentrated contract/project cargo.\\n- [Transport Corporation of India, FY25 report](https://tcil.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Annual-Report-2024-25.pdf): freight and supply-chain customer relationships are relevant; seaways, multimodal assets and scale make its consolidated return/multiple an imperfect benchmark.\\n\\nComparable current valuation metrics are ND here because synchronized peer price, debt, accounting basis and cash restrictions were not reconciled. These peers supply business context, not evidence that Rapid Fleet deserves their economics.\\n\\n## 6. Revenue growth and scale\\n| Window | Start sales | End sales | Nominal CAGR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 3y | 105.52 | 204.35 | 24.65% |\\n| 4y | 113.70 | 204.35 | 15.79% |\\n| 5y | 65.87 | 204.35 | 25.41% |\\n\\nTen-year CAGR is unavailable because the issuer was incorporated in2017. The FY21 five-year nominal base is affected by the pandemic period; the three-year cross-check is therefore important. Neither reported series isolates freight-price pass-through, owned/hired mix, customer wallet share or volume. No acquisition is identified, but absence of an acquisition is not proof that nominal sales growth is clean organic demand. REV remains NS rather than selecting the most flattering window. [E06]\\n\\nFY26 revenue grew18.5%, but the first half fell about11.7% before a strong second half. Q1FY27 management revenue was ₹53.60 crore versus ₹32.93 crore. It is a business update, not a complete audited quarter: TTM revenue can be calculated as FY26 + Q1FY27 − Q1FY26, but TTM PAT, cash flow and current net debt cannot. Single-quarter acceleration does not earn a sustained-trajectory bonus. [Q127]\\n\\nGrowth drivers are more fleet capacity and specialized mix, route/customer additions and utilization; exact volume/price/mix contributions are ND. Value added below provides a gross-billing cross-check but is not audited segment gross profit.\\n\\n| FY | Sales | Value added | Op EBITDA | EBIT | PAT | Normalized PAT | CFO | Cash capex | FCF gross capex |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 65.87 | 11.45 | 4.96 | 4.21 | 2.34 | 2.37 | 6.28 | 3.91 | 2.37 |\\n| 2022 | 113.70 | 14.08 | 8.72 | 5.56 | 3.40 | 3.21 | 24.39 | 16.54 | 7.85 |\\n| 2023 | 105.52 | 17.27 | 12.13 | 7.28 | 4.71 | 4.37 | 7.43 | 3.31 | 4.12 |\\n| 2024 | 115.59 | 23.42 | 16.97 | 11.43 | 8.07 | 7.69 | 6.46 | 8.37 | -1.91 |\\n| 2025 | 172.44 | 28.75 | 20.33 | 12.75 | 10.28 | 7.73 | 0.92 | 17.55 | -16.62 |\\n| 2026 | 204.35 | 35.44 | 26.90 | 17.01 | 12.47 | 11.37 | 7.81 | 25.35 | -17.54 |\\n\\n| FY | Sales growth | ₹ addition | EBITDA margin | PAT margin | ROIC proxy | ROE | Average AR days | End AR days | Current-share EPS |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | NS | NS | 7.53% | 3.56% | NS | NS | NS | 85.12 | 3.15 |\\n| 2022 | 72.61% | 47.83 | 7.67% | 2.99% | 28.61% | 59.06% | 65.80 | 82.28 | 4.57 |\\n| 2023 | -7.19% | -8.18 | 11.50% | 4.47% | 40.28% | 48.36% | 91.54 | 94.43 | 6.34 |\\n| 2024 | 9.54% | 10.06 | 14.68% | 6.98% | 43.36% | 50.09% | 87.89 | 89.56 | 10.86 |\\n| 2025 | 49.19% | 56.85 | 11.79% | 5.96% | 24.18% | 23.03% | 65.80 | 71.57 | 13.83 |\\n| 2026 | 18.51% | 31.91 | 13.16% | 6.10% | 18.45% | 16.55% | 83.90 | 107.40 | 16.77 |\\n\\n## 7. Growth quality and reinvestment\\nOperating EBITDA excludes all other income and includes recurring CSR even when historically presented below operating expenses. Normalized PAT uses operating EBIT less finance cost and25% analytical tax; this is a transparent proxy, not a company-adjusted earnings measure. FY26 prior gratuity ₹0.0302 crore is excluded from the recurring operating calculation. Employee misappropriation is not added back without an identified accounting charge. EPS is rebased to all7,434,800 current shares throughout the comparison; it is not labelled historical statutory diluted EPS.\\n\\nFY25 weighted EPS₹19.97 and FY26₹16.77 do not demonstrate a fall in comparable issued-share earnings. Conversely, the IPO dilution must not be ignored. FY25 other income included ₹2.6095 crore investment gains. FY26 operating EBITDA is ₹26.8976 crore, below management EBITDA₹28.4132 crore, which incorporates non-operating items. [E01,E06,E08]\\n\\n| Window | ΔNOPAT | Δfinancing IC | Incremental proxy | ΔPPE | Δdeposits | Δbroad ONWC | Definition residual |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 3y | 7.29 | 69.90 | 10.43% | 27.00 | 0.12 | 50.90 | -8.12 |\\n| 5y | 9.59 | 67.89 | 14.13% | 38.83 | 0.30 | 37.84 | -9.07 |\\n\\nFY26 detailed operating IC = PPE51.0217 + operating deposits0.4606 + AR60.1300 + supplier/employee/other receivables0.8851 − payables21.3882 − customer/audit accrual0.1006 = ₹91.0086 crore. Financing IC = equity81.5901 + debt17.3994 − CE14.7091 − investment0.0025 = ₹84.2779 crore. Residual ₹-6.7307 crore comprises tax/other non-operating balances, statutory liabilities, provisions, disposal advances and classification differences, rather than unexplained project returns.\\n\\nThe three-year incremental proxy is weaker than the five-year result, so CAP4 reflects deterioration rather than claiming every cohort destroys value. A reasoned nominal INR capital-cost range is13–17%: secured vehicle borrowing near9–10% before tax is cheaper than equity, but equity dominates market financing and faces concentration, SME liquidity and reporting risk. This is an analytical range, not a measured CAPM estimate. Entry-return hurdle15% is a separate shareholder convention.\\n\\nPPE, deposits and broad working capital reconcile to the financing-IC change through the disclosed definition residual. Net cash capex less depreciation is cross-checked against book PPE movement, retaining disposal/book and vintage differences. FY26 PPE reconciles exactly:36.3847 +25.3476 −9.8921 −0.8224 disposal NBV +0.0039 prior excess-DA correction =51.0217 crore; sale proceeds1.0988 less gain0.2764 equal disposal NBV0.8224. Growth ≈ reinvestment × incremental return is a warning check, not a guaranteed identity: fast revenue growth requires capital unless turns or margins improve. Maintenance is undisclosed. Base replacement cash5.5% of sales exceeds book DA4.84%;4.84% and6.5% are tested. Growth productive assets require25–28% of incremental sales in the scenarios. No claim that all capex is discretionary growth is made.\\n\\n## 8. Management, governance and execution\\nAudit chronology matters more than the opinion headline:\\n1. FY21 and FY22: A.K. Lunawath, unmodified financial and effective-IFC opinions; no reported fraud/default. FY22 explicitly reports no auditor resignation that year.\\n2. FY23: Gupta Agarwal successor report records Iyer & Co resignation during that year. Exact intervening appointment, reason and handover documentation remain ND. The later Gupta/Jay Gupta name change is not another resignation. A small₹3,088 professional-tax arrear was reported.\\n3. FY24: unmodified financial/IFC opinions, but audit trail did not operate throughout all transactions; CARO says no commensurate internal audit; ₹9.51 lakh CSR remained unspent. Balance confirmations were subject to adjustments.\\n4. FY25: audit-trail failure recurs; two employees misappropriated₹85 lakh, detected by management with disciplinary/legal action. Recovery, insurance and charge/receivable treatment have not been reconciled. It is not described as promoter theft.\\n5. FY26: audit trail and internal audit reported functioning, no new fraud reported, but secretarial audit finds missing post-listing shareholder approval for material SLB transactions, MD remuneration beyond the specifically approved limit, and a delayed MGT-14. Those findings are not erased by unmodified financial and IFC opinions. [E02,E13]\\n\\nFY26 related-party sales were approximately₹21.529 crore and related-party trade receivables₹7.0585 crore. The report's comparative tables labelled FY25 reproduce FY24 values. Original FY25 SLB sales₹18.674 crore and receivable₹3.7816 crore cannot be replaced by the older labelled figures. A corrected note and counterparty ledger are prerequisites, not evidence of diversion by themselves. FY25 also disclosed an₹8 crore SLB loan and₹0.615 crore interest; mapping movements to closing borrowing categories is required. The deceased-director₹0.1651 crore write-off is distinct from the₹0.85 crore employee event. [E03]\\n\\nFY26 MD/executive remuneration₹0.30/₹0.24 crore is modest relative to PAT, but authorization matters independently of size. September2026 voting passed the remuneration and prospective₹25 crore each SLB/Sri Kamakshi RPT proposals, with promoter abstention on RPT resolutions. This is a positive procedural development, not retrospective proof that FY26 was compliant. Promoters waived their FY26 dividend; public cash distribution is₹0.289596 crore. [E12]\\n\\nIPO gross proceeds are2,284,800 ×₹192 =₹43.86816 crore, not the₹60.14 crore claimed in annual-report narrative. Intended vehicle, working-capital and general-corporate use must be reconciled with actual capex, issue expenses and FD balances. FY25 cash issue expenses₹6.9879 crore and FY26₹0.0246 crore are retained; they are not recurring EBITDA costs. A current complete utilization statement and earmark status are still needed.\\n\\nPromises versus delivery: the prospectus fleet base and subsequently reported additions show investment; FY26 management reports72,689 trips/2.01 crore km, and Q1 a127-tonne ODC delivery. These operational milestones are positives. The order claim₹230 crore has up-to-one-year tenure but no opening/intake/execution/cancellation reconciliation, customer-level margin or minimum-volume guarantee. It is neither guaranteed incremental revenue nor an extra scoring bonus.\\n\\n## 9. Financial forensics, cash and funding\\n| Window | CFO reported | PAT | Conversion | CFO less FY25 investment gains | Adjusted conversion |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2y | 8.73 | 22.75 | 0.384 | 6.12 | 0.269 |\\n| 3y | 15.19 | 30.82 | 0.493 | 12.58 | 0.408 |\\n| 5y | 47.01 | 38.93 | 1.207 | 44.40 | 1.140 |\\n\\nFY26 receivables rose₹26.3168 crore, much faster than sales; ending debtor days rose from about72 to107. Average days and ending days are shown separately. Billed balances older than six months rose from₹1.1759 crore to₹4.9797 crore. Unbilled₹17.0777 crore represents roughly28% of total receivables. The AS9 KAM addresses evidence of rendered services and later invoicing, not guaranteed collection. [E07]\\n\\nFY26 CFO bridge,₹crore: pre-working-capital cash27.4116; advances release4.1091; receivables absorption26.3168; short provisions1.4752; other assets absorption0.5868; payables2.8200; other liabilities3.6770; cash generated12.5894 less tax4.7778 = CFO7.8116. Statutory liabilities and a capital-sale advance are not customer deposits. Cash capex25.3476 less disposal receipts1.0988, deposits/margin movements and interest produce CFI−23.8290; CFF−9.3500 gives cash decline25.3674 to14.7091, within source rounding. Working capital is not deducted again from CFO.\\n\\nFY25 printed CFO includes investment gains not removed in the reconciliation. Analytical reclassification moves₹2.6095 crore from CFO to CFI: CFO−₹1.6891 crore and CFI−₹9.6233 crore, with total cash unchanged. This is a classification sensitivity, not a restated audit opinion or verified sale-proceeds ledger. FY24 original CFO₹6.6035 crore, prospectus₹6.4594 crore and AR25 comparative₹6.4661 crore are retained; security-deposit, disposal and cash/FD classifications differ. FY21 CFO also includes current-debt movement inside other liabilities; analytical removal₹1.3294004 crore prevents calling it all customer cash. Five-year cash conversion does not excuse the weaker recent three-year record.\\n\\nMarch2026 debt₹17.3994 crore consists of vehicles₹12.9101 crore and OD₹4.4893 crore;₹7.4734 crore vehicle maturities are current. Cash equivalents₹14.7091 crore include liquid FD₹12.2691 crore; margin FD₹0.4937 crore is excluded. Available IPO-earmarked liquidity is not independently established, so exclusion sensitivities are explicit. Government balances are not presumed instantly refundable. Opening tax/statutory settlement is stressed separately from future income tax, without double-counting operating WC.\\n\\nThe primary Q1 announcement confirms some bank financing but no amount, interest, term, security or covenant schedule. Therefore October net cash is not known. Model pre-entry financing amounts0,4,8.645 and12.35 crore are assumptions, not disclosures. Existing vehicle amortization is allocated from audited current maturities; remaining debt is conservatively allocated over two years. Q1 financing is tested as48-month debt at10.5%, not asserted contractual terms. OD renewal and additional headroom are explicitly uncommitted. No bank-rating search result is interpreted as proof that the company is unrated.\\n\\n## 10. Valuation and market expectations\\n₹208.50 × 7,434,800 shares = ₹155.01558 crore. FY26 reported P/E 12.43x; normalized P/E 13.63x; P/B 1.90x. March-anchored EV ₹157.71 crore and EV/operating EBITDA 5.86x are stale-balance diagnostics, not current EV. Mean/median60-session traded value ₹1904736.00/₹1332600.00. Reference ₹5 lakh exit at5% participation 7.504 sessions; at2% 18.760 and10% 3.752. One600-sharelot₹125,100;three₹375,300;four₹500,400.\\n\\nForty-five issuer rows occur across60 exchange sessions;15 absent rows are treated as zero capacity, not falsely certified zero trades. All latest20 sessions have issuer rows. Execution assumes participation, not guaranteed fills. The static master price range and market-maker fields are not observed bid–ask spreads. L starts5 under the prescribed schedule and receives an explicit one-point implementation haircut for missing tape days and lot granularity.\\n\\n### Linked valuation design\\nThe model begins31 March2026, bridges202 days to19 October, then runs60 monthly periods and a12-month terminal continuation. Q1 revenue/capex are primary management facts; monthly allocation and all remaining activity, margin, turnover and timing inputs are assumptions. Four intramonth cycles pay80% of hired-trip costs before collection. Gross collections less gross operating payments exactly reconcile to EBITDA less ΔONWC. Capex, principal, interest and tax are actual model cash events, not year-end plugs. One/two/eight-cycle timing sensitivities are retained.\\n\\nOptional rescue: up to₹5.5107 crore analyst-sized bank headroom at12%, and up to₹15 crore gross equity at₹156.375,25% below entry, with3% fee. Equity uses ceiling whole-share issuance and is additionally bounded by10 million authorized shares. No pre-entry equity is invented, because current issued shares are verified. No rescue is injected after the first unfunded breach. Bank renewal is not equivalent to a committed facility. Cash breaches and financing triggers use the same 1e-24 crore numerical tolerance, so Decimal arithmetic residue alone never causes a bank draw or one-share equity issue; this is not an economic funding allowance.\\n\\nThe illustrative conditional bridge assumes₹8.645 crore Q1 bank finance and pre-entry DSO85; neither is verified. It is compared to the no-quantified-draw bridge, not adopted as the actual opening balance sheet. A lower entry price does not cure company funding failure, and a lower future issue price creates more dilution.\\n\\n| Case | First breach | New shares | Terminal cash required / available | Conditional terminal price | IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Unresolved-entry bear no_new_finance | 2026-05-31 opening_statutory_payable | 0 | 4.54 / 3.00 | NS | NS |\\n| Conditional-bank/collections bear no_new_finance | 2027-03-19 OD_due_no_assumed_renewal | 0 | 4.54 / 1.52 | NS | NS |\\n| Unresolved-entry bear bounded_optional | 2026-05-31 cycle_1_80pct_hire_pre_payment | 0 | 14.54 / 8.81 | NS | NS |\\n| Conditional-bank/collections bear bounded_optional | None | 591106 | 14.54 / 16.74 | 49.60 | -24.96% |\\n| Unresolved-entry base no_new_finance | 2026-05-31 opening_statutory_payable | 0 | 7.10 / 6.25 | NS | NS |\\n| Conditional-bank/collections base no_new_finance | 2027-03-19 cycle_1_80pct_hire_pre_payment | 0 | 7.10 / 4.76 | NS | NS |\\n| Unresolved-entry base bounded_optional | 2026-06-30 existing_vehicle_principal | 0 | 17.10 / 12.06 | NS | NS |\\n| Conditional-bank/collections base bounded_optional | None | 409475 | 17.10 / 17.51 | 201.78 | -0.65% |\\n| Unresolved-entry bull no_new_finance | 2026-05-31 opening_statutory_payable | 0 | 9.80 / 28.64 | NS | NS |\\n| Conditional-bank/collections bull no_new_finance | 2027-11-19 cycle_1_80pct_hire_pre_payment | 0 | 9.80 / 27.15 | NS | NS |\\n| Unresolved-entry bull bounded_optional | 2026-06-30 existing_vehicle_principal | 0 | 19.80 / 34.44 | NS | NS |\\n| Conditional-bank/collections bull bounded_optional | None | 0 | 18.84 / 33.51 | 482.33 | 18.26% |\\n\\n### Conditional bear annual bridge\\n| Year | Sales | EBITDA | NOPAT | CFO | Capex | ΔONWC | FCFF | Interest | Principal | End cash | End debt | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | 205.00 | 20.50 | 7.93 | 2.41 | 11.27 | 15.95 | -12.37 | 2.08 | 7.08 | 3.32 | 19.61 | 7939206 |\\n| 2 | 205.00 | 20.50 | 7.93 | 18.31 | 11.27 | 0.00 | 6.58 | 1.84 | 4.88 | 4.96 | 14.73 | 8025906 |\\n| 3 | 210.00 | 22.05 | 8.91 | 18.20 | 11.55 | 1.22 | 6.31 | 1.39 | 3.29 | 6.93 | 11.44 | 8025906 |\\n| 4 | 215.00 | 22.58 | 9.13 | 18.60 | 11.83 | 1.22 | 6.48 | 1.14 | 1.44 | 11.11 | 10.00 | 8025906 |\\n| 5 | 220.00 | 23.10 | 9.34 | 19.04 | 12.31 | 1.22 | 6.45 | 1.09 | 0.00 | 16.74 | 10.00 | 8025906 |\\n\\n### Conditional base annual bridge\\n| Year | Sales | EBITDA | NOPAT | CFO | Capex | ΔONWC | FCFF | Interest | Principal | End cash | End debt | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | 240.00 | 31.68 | 15.05 | 15.85 | 16.61 | 11.25 | -2.23 | 1.75 | 7.08 | 4.80 | 16.05 | 7434800 |\\n| 2 | 275.00 | 37.13 | 17.86 | 25.33 | 24.58 | 6.28 | 0.25 | 1.79 | 4.88 | 5.95 | 14.73 | 7670569 |\\n| 3 | 310.00 | 42.78 | 20.83 | 29.90 | 26.50 | 6.28 | 2.98 | 1.39 | 3.29 | 6.69 | 11.44 | 7809014 |\\n| 4 | 345.00 | 48.30 | 23.70 | 34.40 | 28.43 | 6.28 | 5.61 | 1.14 | 1.44 | 10.54 | 10.00 | 7844275 |\\n| 5 | 380.00 | 53.20 | 26.11 | 38.49 | 30.35 | 6.28 | 7.79 | 1.09 | 0.00 | 17.51 | 10.00 | 7844275 |\\n\\n### Conditional bull annual bridge\\n| Year | Sales | EBITDA | NOPAT | CFO | Capex | ΔONWC | FCFF | Interest | Principal | End cash | End debt | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | 270.00 | 37.80 | 18.55 | 26.71 | 20.96 | 5.34 | 5.24 | 1.71 | 7.08 | 11.50 | 14.10 | 7434800 |\\n| 2 | 330.00 | 49.50 | 25.15 | 33.41 | 33.15 | 8.00 | -0.16 | 1.21 | 4.88 | 7.19 | 10.86 | 7434800 |\\n| 3 | 400.00 | 64.00 | 33.48 | 43.80 | 39.50 | 9.34 | 3.86 | 1.20 | 3.29 | 9.29 | 10.01 | 7434800 |\\n| 4 | 470.00 | 77.55 | 41.10 | 54.77 | 43.35 | 9.34 | 11.01 | 1.03 | 1.44 | 18.56 | 9.04 | 7434800 |\\n| 5 | 550.00 | 93.50 | 50.16 | 66.35 | 50.25 | 10.67 | 15.69 | 0.97 | 0.00 | 33.51 | 9.04 | 7434800 |\\n\\nConditional return attribution (price factor = normalized-profit factor × share-count factor × implied P/E factor):\\n| Case | Profit factor | Share factor | Implied P/E factor | Price factor |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | 0.75 | 0.93 | 0.34 | 0.24 |\\n| base | 2.22 | 0.95 | 0.46 | 0.97 |\\n| bull | 4.35 | 1.00 | 0.53 | 2.31 |\\n\\nProductive fixed capital follows an economic target, not an asserted GAAP NBV forecast. Cash maintenance above book DA is an explicit replacement/retirement gap: opening productive assets + capex − DA − gap = closing productive assets. The gap is retained monthly and annually. It represents replacement cost beyond historical book depreciation, not free new capacity.\\n\\nEarned terminal EV = next-year FCFF / (discount rate − growth), using operating NOPAT, working-capital growth, productive-asset/deposit growth, replacement above DA and growing retained cash buffer. Base discount15%, growth3%; discount13/17% and growth0/4% tested. Terminal debt is settled once, and a fixed-point buffer funds12 months of operating cycles and reinvestment without new finance. Positive recurring FCFF and recurring cash are required. A loss-making continuation with enough cash for one year cannot earn an ordinary going-concern return. EV plus cash less debt and retained buffer is divided by diluted shares.4/6/8x EBITDA cross-checks are assumptions, not claimed peer multiples. The DCF diagnostic is not a shareholder NPV if future outside equity finances growth.\\n\\n| Test | Status | Target price | Margin bracket | Feasible high price |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 12pct | cash-feasible price root | 367.45 | 15.00%–15.00% | 367.45 |\\n| 15pct | cash-feasible price root | 419.37 | 15.74%–15.74% | 419.37 |\\n| 18pct | cash-feasible price root | 477.00 | 16.59%–16.59% | 477.00 |\\n| fivefold | cash-feasible price root | 1042.50 | 24.90%–24.90% | 1042.50 |\\n| tenfold | no feasible root within6–35% margin bound | 2085.00 | 6.00%–35.00% | NS |\\n\\nA price root is labelled only when both endpoint paths survive and their price residuals bracket zero. Otherwise the result is a joint feasibility threshold, possibly driven by cash or dilution discontinuity. Every root starts with a bounded scan before bisection; failed cash tests and all trials remain in the canonical record.12/15/18% entry limits are conditional algebraic hurdle values, not buy prices. Two-way margin/DSO, discount/replacement, growth, issue-price, bank-headroom, earmark and timing sensitivities are available. No probability weighting is applied to unsupported financing paths.\\n\\n## 11. Bear case and permanent-loss risks\\nThe bear case combines lower margin, slower collections, receivable cash loss and replacement investment. It can require dilution even if eventual accounting profit remains positive. Capital can be permanently impaired through uncollectible unbilled/RPT balances, loss of a large customer, stranded specialized equipment or expensive rescue issuance. This is different from temporary share-price volatility.\\n\\nCollateral sale values, recovery priority, accident insurance and lender enforcement costs are insufficient for a credible liquidation floor. Book PPE is not cash at par. Historical accident/collection proceedings in the prospectus are not proven closed by a later general audit statement that no litigation affects financial position. No criminal conviction or promoter fraud is inferred. [E14]\\n\\n## 12. Fivefold and tenfold feasibility\\n₹208.50 requires terminal₹1,042.50 for5x and₹2,085 for10x before shareholder dividends. These imply roughly38.0% and58.5% five-year price CAGRs, not ordinary bull outcomes. The reverse solver tests those prices with the same pre-entry cash, hire prepayments, growth capital, loan repayment, bounded whole-share dilution and earned terminal reinvestment. A missing feasible root is retained as failure, not extrapolated beyond the35% margin bound.\\n\\nBottom-up throughput equivalents are retained for each year: FY26 gross realization and reported2.01 crore km/72,689 trips translate scenario sales into equivalent km/trips; a300-vehicle equivalent at Q1 annualized sales is also shown. These are not physical capacity forecasts because reported trips include hired vehicles, and own/hired utilization is ND. The bull reaches₹550 crore sales, demanding much larger accessible customer volumes, working capital and productive assets. No unverifiable national-market share is used as permission to assume that scale.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring dashboard\\n1. Obtain corrected FY26 RPT comparative notes and reconcile SLB/Sri Kamakshi balances with original FY25 ledgers.\\n2. Obtain the October cash/debt bridge, Q1 vehicle loan sanctions, repayment schedules and actual draw amounts before assigning BAL/R.\\n3. Track unbilled-to-billed conversion and cash collection; investigate ending AR days above100 and older-than-six-month balances above₹5 crore.\\n4. Reconcile the₹85 lakh employee event: recognized loss, recovery, insurance and documented control remediation.\\n5. Verify audit-trail coverage, independent internal audit and timely RPT/remuneration approvals; another recurrence weakens G1.\\n6. Require current fleet count, additions/disposals, age, owned/hired revenue and replacement budget; reconcile claimed fleet NBV to audited PPE.\\n7. Obtain IPO utilization and FD-earmark schedules; do not count restricted funds as distributable liquidity.\\n8. Reconcile orders to remaining executable revenue, margins, advances and cancellation rights; track recognized sales separately.\\n9. Seek sustained CFO/PAT above0.8 with no classification boost or supplier stretching before lifting CASH3.\\n10. Refresh60-session liquidity, lot size and actual exchange restrictions before any implementation decision.\\n\\n## 14. Final classification and conditional action\\n**Avoid — governance, with a separate unresolved-data Hold.** Governance cap and missing organic-growth/current-funding inputs prevent a defensible composite investment score. This is not a claim that the business is worthless or insolvent. It is a refusal to treat a conditional upside model as a financeable shareholder outcome.\\n\\nReconsider only after corrected disclosures, bank/collection evidence and durable operating cash conversion. Recompute the whole model rather than changing only the P/E. No unconditional buy range, position size or guaranteed liquidity is offered. Scenario limits are decision diagnostics, not personalized financial advice.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, assumptions and open questions\\n| ID | Source / date | Reviewed scope |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| AR26 | [FY2025–26 annual report](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31280_RAPIDFLEET_2025_2026_A_19045203_03092026184433.pdf) 2026-09-03 | PDF123–134 complete financial audit/CARO/IFC;135–162 accounts and notes;106–113 complete secretarial report/annexure;41–72 AGM notice;76–103 directors and MD&A |\\n| AR25 | [FY2024–25 annual report](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_28974_RAPIDFLEET_2024_2025_A_6055495_08092025213555.pdf) 2025-09-08 | PDF90–104 complete financial audit/CARO/IFC;105–135 accounts/notes and original RPT; directors report and IPO narrative |\\n| AR24 | [Original FY2023–24 annual report](https://rapidfleet.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Annual-Report_23-24_compressed-1.pdf) 2024-08-08 | PDF9–18 complete statutory audit/IFC/CARO;19–40 statements/notes. Audit trail, internal audit and CSR adverse observations explicitly retained. |\\n| AR23 | [Original FY2022–23 annual report and filed accounts](https://rapidfleet.in/wp-content/uploads/pdfs/AnnualReport/Annual%20Report_22-23-min.pdf) 2023-09-30 | PDF29–38 complete financial audit/IFC/CARO; financials and notes, original-versus-restated bridge |\\n| AR22 | [Original FY2021–22 annual report](https://rapidfleet.in/wp-content/uploads/pdfs/AnnualReport/Annual%20Report_21-22-min.pdf) 2022-09-05 | PDF9–21 complete statutory audit/CARO/IFC;22–35 financial statements and notes;restatement differences preserved. |\\n| AR21 | [Original FY2020–21 annual report](https://rapidfleet.in/wp-content/uploads/pdfs/AnnualReport/Annual%20Report_20-21-min.pdf) 2021-11-30 | PDF10–19 complete statutory audit/CARO/IFC;20–32 financials and notes. Revenue/DA/capex visually checked;current debt in OCL separately identified. |\\n| PROSPECTUS | [Final IPO prospectus](https://rapidfleet.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Rapid-Fleet_Prospectu.pdf) 2025-03-26 | PDF risk factors25–50;IPOobjects and sharecapital;business123–146;restatementaudit211–214;financials215–237;litigation. Restatement report not treated as original annual audit. |\\n| FY26UPDATE | [FY26 performance and fleet update](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPIDFLEET_22052026093226_pr220526__1___1_.pdf) 2026-05-22 | All5pages;management measures distinguished from audited operating EBITDA. Company August investor-corner copy byte-identical, not a new update. |\\n| Q127 | [Q1FY27 business update](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPIDFLEET_31082026085830_business_merged.pdf) 2026-08-31 | All6pages;Q1revenue53.60vs32.93, capex12.35, bank financing amount ND, vehicle and orders;not audited quarter P&L or balance sheet |\\n| DIV | [Promoter dividend waiver](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPIDFLEET_23092026194401_RFMSLBoardMeetingOutcome23092026Signed.pdf) 2026-09-23 | Both pages: irrevocable FY26 promoter waiver;public entitlement unchanged;18Sep record date |\\n| AGM | [AGM proceedings](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPIDFLEET_25092026204100_RFMSLAGMProceedings25092026FinalSigned.pdf) 2026-09-25 | All5pages;agenda/waiver, no speaker requests;voting confirmation separately sourced |\\n| VOTE | [AGM voting and scrutinizer report](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPIDFLEET_28092026175031_SCRUTINIZERR.pdf) 2026-09-28 | All8 resolutions;RPTpromoter5,021,500 abstentions;prospectiveFY27 caps passed |\\n| PEER_TCI | [Transport Corporation of India FY25 annual report](https://tcil.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Annual-Report-2024-25.pdf) FY2025, available before cutoff | Business model and segment scope only, not a full peer financial audit |\\n| PEER_VRL | [VRL Logistics FY25 annual report](https://www.vrlgroup.in/investor_download/Annual_Report_2024_25.pdf) FY2025, available before cutoff | Business model and segment scope only, not a full peer financial audit |\\n| PEER_RITCO | [Ritco Logistics FY25 annual report](https://www.ritcologistics.com/frontassets/images/Annual_Report_2024-2025.pdf) FY2025, available before cutoff | Issuer presentation business scope: bulk/FTL, owned and aggregated vehicles, warehousing; no contemporary fleet count or peer multiple inferred. FY25 annual report indexed scope cross-check only. |\\n\\nDated NSE security-master and all60 issuer-specific bhavcopy extracts, public URLs and original-byte hashes are in the source-safe inputs. The framework filename, version, byte count and hash are retained. No source PDF, page image, raw extraction dump, transfer helper or private storage identifier is included.\\n\\nThe canonical dataset retains 213 deduplicated full paths and all calculation rows, including failed roots,67 monthly/pre-entry records per path, intraperiod events and terminal events. Minimal authored inputs and standard-library code suffice for offline reconstruction. Verification checks arithmetic, hashes, event recurrence, bounded finance, score caps/NS, path references, cash survival and terminal continuing capital. A clean recovery deletes generated outputs and old attestations before rebuilding under a read/network guard; verification output is regenerated, not trusted from a copied badge.\\n\\nOpen items are substantive: exact organic demand, current customer concentration, maintenance capex, asset unit economics, original-to-restated account reconciliations, employee-loss accounting, historical auditor-transition reasons, current bank terms, IPO earmarks and legal-case status. None is silently filled with management optimism.\\n\"","\"number\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"Executive verdict\"","\"**Avoid — governance; separately Hold — unresolved data. Exclude from investable rankings.** Rapid Fleet has genuinely expanded its road-transport business and is developing specialized over-dimensional cargo capabilities. But gross sales growth is not yet a clean measure of underlying demand, earnings have converted poorly into cash recently, and governance concerns recur across several audit years. This is a mixed-quality growth/special-situation thesis, not an evidence-backed compounder. A low headline P/E does not compensate automatically for uncertain entry liquidity and disclosure reconciliation.\\n\\nThe decisive facts are ₹60.13 crore receivables, including ₹17.0777 crore unbilled, ₹25.3476 crore FY26 cash capex, and another ₹12.35 crore Q1FY27 capex partly funded by bank finance whose amount and terms are undisclosed. The complete model preserves pre-entry, intramonth and terminal funding failures. A subsequently positive cash balance never repairs an earlier failed return path. [E02–E10]\\n\\n\"","\"Research header, gates and data confidence\"","\"- Exact issuer: Rapid Fleet Management Services Limited, NSE Emerge RAPIDFLEET, ISIN INE0QX901013. It is not Rapid Multimodal Logistics. Incorporated 26 December 2017; public conversion in August 2023. Group/business-origin narratives are not issuer incorporation dates.\\n- Research cutoff: 20 October 2026. Price date: 19 October 2026. Five-year investment clock: 19 October 2026 to 19 October 2031. Later retrieval does not make later information admissible.\\n- Standalone Indian GAAP, Accounting Standards (AS), including AS9, not Ind AS. No subsidiaries, associates or joint ventures in the reviewed scope. One directors-report reference to Ind AS24 does not change the audited accounting basis.\\n- INR crore unless specified. Six fiscal observations FY21–FY26; original annual audit reports and their annexures read, with FY22–24 restated financial series separately retained. FY21 original, FY22–24 IPO restatement, FY25 original and FY26 current vintages are labelled, not silently spliced into a claim of identical restatement scope.\\n- **G1 Conditional:** repeated stewardship/control deficiencies; no demonstrated promoter diversion or severe fraud allegation. GOV3 activates the framework cap and the Avoid — governance classification, independently of whether Gate1 is Fail.\\n- **G2 Hold:** incorrect RPT comparative labels, cash-flow vintage/classification differences, and unverified October cash/new debt materially limit the investment conclusion.\\n- **G3 Conditional:** audited March leverage is manageable, but required financing/collections are not established at entry. Conditional model failure is not proof of actual default.\\n- **G4 Conditional:** 600-share lot and meaningful exit friction. No suspension asserted; current spread and ASM/GSM status remain ND.\\n\\nConfidence is high for audited headline FY25–26 accounts and the dated market tape, medium for historical normalized economics, low for current liquidity, organic volume growth and unconditional return valuation. NS means not scoreable; ND means not disclosed/established; neither means zero.\\n\\n\"","\"Scorecard and calculation audit\"","\"| Composite | Known sum | Coverage | Raw | Raw bound | Cap | Final / capped bound |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | 3.18 | 80.00% | NS | ['3.38', '5.18'] | 3 | NS / ['3.00', '3.00'] |\\n| GQS | 2.85 | 60.00% | NS | ['3.25', '6.85'] | 5 | NS / ['3.25', '5.00'] |\\n| IAS | 1.70 | 40.00% | NS | ['2.80', '5.95'] | 3 | NS / ['2.80', '3.00'] |\\n\\nCanonical factors are assigned once and reused. Weights: BQS GOV20%, MOAT20%, REV12%, DUR10%, CAP12%, CASH10%, BAL8%, EXEC8%; GQS REV40%, DUR20%, EARN15%, CAP15%, CASH10%; IAS BQS25%, R35%, D20%, P10%, L10%. Known contributions and every missing weight are preserved. Bound = known sum + missing weight × [1,10], with no renormalization. Decimal precision32 and half-up one-decimal display are used. IAS additionally propagates the already-capped BQS bounds rather than treating its NS input as an unconstrained1–10. Bounds are mathematical limits, not estimated scores. Even a collapsed capped bound does not turn NS into a point score.\\n\\nGOV3 caps BQS and IAS at3; persistent CASH3 caps GQS at5. G1 and G3 are not mechanically marked Fail. GOV±1 and CAP±1 sensitivities, including the discontinuous disappearance of the governance cap at GOV4, are in the calculation audit. The return hurdle is nominal15% annually;12% and18% sensitivities are explicit. Conditional model IRRs are not substituted for R.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence | Basis / contrary evidence |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 3 | Medium | E02, E03, E12, E13 | Recurring control and authorization failures: FY24 no commensurate internal audit, FY24–25 incomplete audit trail, FY25 employee misappropriation, FY26 material RPT approval and MD pay-limit breaches, plus mislabelled RPT comparatives. No promoter diversion alleged. Contrary: Financial and IFC opinions are unmodified. Promoter dividend waiver and subsequent prospective RPT approvals are positives, but do not erase historical lapses. |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium | E04, E05 | Bidding-based road transport and hired capacity are replicable. High historical customer concentration limits bargaining power. Contrary: Specialized ODC execution and customer relationships are useful, but excess returns and switching costs are unverified. |\\n| REV | NS | Low | E06, E04 | The preselected five-year organic window and three-year fallback cannot be isolated from fuel pass-through and owned/hired mix. Nominal reported CAGRs remain diagnostics; underlying REV is NS. Contrary: Reported sales and value added expanded materially. No acquisition was identified, but a clean demand bridge is missing. |\\n| DUR | 6 | Medium | E11, E05 | Existing auto, renewable and project-cargo customers provide accessible demand. The fleet investment and short-tenor orders demonstrate real commercial activity. Contrary: Order cancellations, remaining backlog and current customer concentration are ND; capex alone does not prove future volumes. |\\n| CAP | 4 | Medium | E10, E07 | The recent three-year incremental return proxy is below the analytical13–17% cost-of-capital range. Fleet and receivable investment absorbed substantial capital. Contrary: The five-year proxy is stronger and new fleet cohorts are immature; aggregate returns are not project IRRs. |\\n| CASH | 3 | High | E07, E08 | Recent three-year CFO/PAT is weak; aged and unbilled receivables increased. FY25 printed CFO retains investment gains. Contrary: FY22–23 conversion was stronger and FY26 CFO was positive, although supplier credit materially supported FY22. |\\n| BAL | NS | Low | E09, E14 | October cash/debt and the amount and terms of Q1 bank finance are unknown. Linked scenarios cannot establish actual funding resilience, so BAL is NS. Contrary: March leverage was modest, cash equivalents existed, and audits report no loan default. Gate3 is not automatically Fail. |\\n| EXEC | 5 | Medium | E11, E04 | Reported trip activity, fleet additions and complex ODC delivery demonstrate operating execution. No formal earnings target is invented. Contrary: Fleet rollforward, customer cohorts and collection outcomes remain unreconciled; operational progress is not cash-conversion excellence. |\\n| EARN | 5 | Medium | E06, E08, E01 | FY26 operating profit and normalized current-share earnings improved. FY25 investment gains and IPO weighted-share EPS complicate comparisons. Contrary: Operating EBITDA increased and the other-income contribution fell; earnings growth is not solely non-operating. |\\n| R | NS | Low | E09, E07 | No defensible unconditional five-year IRR exists while entry funding is unknown. Conditional rescue outcomes are not substituted into R. Contrary: The model retains feasible and failed conditional returns and explicit12/15/18% hurdles. |\\n| D | 4 | Medium | E05, E07, E09 | Customer concentration, unbilled/aged receivables and upfront hire funding expose permanent capital loss. A rescued bear case may still dilute and lose value. Contrary: Tangible fleet and March liquidity provide some protection; actual insolvency is not established. |\\n| P | 5 | Medium | E06, E09 | Audited FY26 P/E is about12.4x and does not plainly require exceptional growth. Removing other income raises the earnings multiple. Contrary: March cash is not current excess cash; an inexpensive headline multiple cannot resolve funding or governance risk. |\\n| L | 4 | High | E15 | The prescribed7.504-session reference exit starts atL5. A disclosed one-point implementation haircut reflects15 absent issuer rows and600-share lot granularity. Contrary: All latest20 sessions contain trades and median turnover is similar; the stock is not labelled untradeable. |\\n\\n\"","\"Business overview and economic engine\"","\"Rapid Fleet provides full/partial truckload, EXIM inland transportation and specialized project cargo. Its economic model combines owned trucks, attached vehicles and market-hired capacity. It is neither a pure software marketplace nor a wholly owned-fleet operator. In FY26 hire charges were ₹126.6049 crore, about62% of sales; direct freight, handling and other operating expenses add further cost. Hire charges are purchased transport services, not automatically finance leases. Offices/yards are rented; AS accounting does not justify importing an invented Ind AS lease liability. [E04]\\n\\nThe prospectus describes paying80% of hired-trip value before dispatch, with proof-of-delivery and billing preceding customer collection. Consequently, a profitable trip can consume cash well before it creates cash. Owned vehicles add maintenance, insurance, driver costs, replacement and secured truck amortization. Detailed owned/hired sales, EBITDA, utilization, asset ages, disposal values and maintenance-versus-growth capex are ND. A blended EBITDA margin cannot establish either fleet model's unit profitability.\\n\\nFleet observations:226 owned at the March2025 prospectus;250+ in the FY26 update; over300 owned claimed at Q1FY27, with25 additions and ₹12.35 crore capex. These are not an exact opening + additions − disposals reconciliation. Bodies, trailers, cranes and reefers complicate unit definitions. The model does not pretend that a250+ to300+ difference equals an audited50-truck purchase.\\n\\n\"","\"Moat, industry and competitive position\"","\"Useful capabilities include route planning, multi-state permits, safe heavy-cargo execution and repeat corporate relationships. These can win difficult assignments. But road freight is fragmented and tender/rate driven; third-party trucks can be sourced by competitors, and customers can rebid routes. Fuel escalation is partial and contract specific. FY24 top five customers were90.40% of revenue and top ten97.70%; H1FY25 top five84.14% and top ten96.59%. Tamil Nadu supplied58.27% of FY24 revenue. Current FY26 customer concentration is not established. [E05]\\n\\nThe prospectus identifies a third-party technology provider, while later marketing describes a proprietary TMS. Without ownership, cost advantage or retention evidence, software is an operating tool, not a scored intellectual-property moat. Large logistics peers can offer integrated networks; small road carriers offer price competition. No unsourced current peer valuation or broad national TAM is used to justify a premium.\\n\\nBusiness-comparability peer set, not a multiple-transfer screen:\\n- [Ritco Logistics, issuer presentation](https://ritcologistics.com/frontassets/images/Ritco_Presentation.pdf): bulk/FTL logistics with owned and aggregated capacity is economically relevant; warehousing and its broader aggregation platform limit direct margin comparison.\\n- [VRL Logistics, FY25 report](https://www.vrlgroup.in/investor_download/Annual_Report_2024_25.pdf): owned-fleet road transportation informs replacement economics, but its large LTL hub-and-spoke parcel network differs from concentrated contract/project cargo.\\n- [Transport Corporation of India, FY25 report](https://tcil.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Annual-Report-2024-25.pdf): freight and supply-chain customer relationships are relevant; seaways, multimodal assets and scale make its consolidated return/multiple an imperfect benchmark.\\n\\nComparable current valuation metrics are ND here because synchronized peer price, debt, accounting basis and cash restrictions were not reconciled. These peers supply business context, not evidence that Rapid Fleet deserves their economics.\\n\\n\"","\"Revenue growth and scale\"","\"| Window | Start sales | End sales | Nominal CAGR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 3y | 105.52 | 204.35 | 24.65% |\\n| 4y | 113.70 | 204.35 | 15.79% |\\n| 5y | 65.87 | 204.35 | 25.41% |\\n\\nTen-year CAGR is unavailable because the issuer was incorporated in2017. The FY21 five-year nominal base is affected by the pandemic period; the three-year cross-check is therefore important. Neither reported series isolates freight-price pass-through, owned/hired mix, customer wallet share or volume. No acquisition is identified, but absence of an acquisition is not proof that nominal sales growth is clean organic demand. REV remains NS rather than selecting the most flattering window. [E06]\\n\\nFY26 revenue grew18.5%, but the first half fell about11.7% before a strong second half. Q1FY27 management revenue was ₹53.60 crore versus ₹32.93 crore. It is a business update, not a complete audited quarter: TTM revenue can be calculated as FY26 + Q1FY27 − Q1FY26, but TTM PAT, cash flow and current net debt cannot. Single-quarter acceleration does not earn a sustained-trajectory bonus. [Q127]\\n\\nGrowth drivers are more fleet capacity and specialized mix, route/customer additions and utilization; exact volume/price/mix contributions are ND. Value added below provides a gross-billing cross-check but is not audited segment gross profit.\\n\\n| FY | Sales | Value added | Op EBITDA | EBIT | PAT | Normalized PAT | CFO | Cash capex | FCF gross capex |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 65.87 | 11.45 | 4.96 | 4.21 | 2.34 | 2.37 | 6.28 | 3.91 | 2.37 |\\n| 2022 | 113.70 | 14.08 | 8.72 | 5.56 | 3.40 | 3.21 | 24.39 | 16.54 | 7.85 |\\n| 2023 | 105.52 | 17.27 | 12.13 | 7.28 | 4.71 | 4.37 | 7.43 | 3.31 | 4.12 |\\n| 2024 | 115.59 | 23.42 | 16.97 | 11.43 | 8.07 | 7.69 | 6.46 | 8.37 | -1.91 |\\n| 2025 | 172.44 | 28.75 | 20.33 | 12.75 | 10.28 | 7.73 | 0.92 | 17.55 | -16.62 |\\n| 2026 | 204.35 | 35.44 | 26.90 | 17.01 | 12.47 | 11.37 | 7.81 | 25.35 | -17.54 |\\n\\n| FY | Sales growth | ₹ addition | EBITDA margin | PAT margin | ROIC proxy | ROE | Average AR days | End AR days | Current-share EPS |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | NS | NS | 7.53% | 3.56% | NS | NS | NS | 85.12 | 3.15 |\\n| 2022 | 72.61% | 47.83 | 7.67% | 2.99% | 28.61% | 59.06% | 65.80 | 82.28 | 4.57 |\\n| 2023 | -7.19% | -8.18 | 11.50% | 4.47% | 40.28% | 48.36% | 91.54 | 94.43 | 6.34 |\\n| 2024 | 9.54% | 10.06 | 14.68% | 6.98% | 43.36% | 50.09% | 87.89 | 89.56 | 10.86 |\\n| 2025 | 49.19% | 56.85 | 11.79% | 5.96% | 24.18% | 23.03% | 65.80 | 71.57 | 13.83 |\\n| 2026 | 18.51% | 31.91 | 13.16% | 6.10% | 18.45% | 16.55% | 83.90 | 107.40 | 16.77 |\\n\\n\"","\"Growth quality and reinvestment\"","\"Operating EBITDA excludes all other income and includes recurring CSR even when historically presented below operating expenses. Normalized PAT uses operating EBIT less finance cost and25% analytical tax; this is a transparent proxy, not a company-adjusted earnings measure. FY26 prior gratuity ₹0.0302 crore is excluded from the recurring operating calculation. Employee misappropriation is not added back without an identified accounting charge. EPS is rebased to all7,434,800 current shares throughout the comparison; it is not labelled historical statutory diluted EPS.\\n\\nFY25 weighted EPS₹19.97 and FY26₹16.77 do not demonstrate a fall in comparable issued-share earnings. Conversely, the IPO dilution must not be ignored. FY25 other income included ₹2.6095 crore investment gains. FY26 operating EBITDA is ₹26.8976 crore, below management EBITDA₹28.4132 crore, which incorporates non-operating items. [E01,E06,E08]\\n\\n| Window | ΔNOPAT | Δfinancing IC | Incremental proxy | ΔPPE | Δdeposits | Δbroad ONWC | Definition residual |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 3y | 7.29 | 69.90 | 10.43% | 27.00 | 0.12 | 50.90 | -8.12 |\\n| 5y | 9.59 | 67.89 | 14.13% | 38.83 | 0.30 | 37.84 | -9.07 |\\n\\nFY26 detailed operating IC = PPE51.0217 + operating deposits0.4606 + AR60.1300 + supplier/employee/other receivables0.8851 − payables21.3882 − customer/audit accrual0.1006 = ₹91.0086 crore. Financing IC = equity81.5901 + debt17.3994 − CE14.7091 − investment0.0025 = ₹84.2779 crore. Residual ₹-6.7307 crore comprises tax/other non-operating balances, statutory liabilities, provisions, disposal advances and classification differences, rather than unexplained project returns.\\n\\nThe three-year incremental proxy is weaker than the five-year result, so CAP4 reflects deterioration rather than claiming every cohort destroys value. A reasoned nominal INR capital-cost range is13–17%: secured vehicle borrowing near9–10% before tax is cheaper than equity, but equity dominates market financing and faces concentration, SME liquidity and reporting risk. This is an analytical range, not a measured CAPM estimate. Entry-return hurdle15% is a separate shareholder convention.\\n\\nPPE, deposits and broad working capital reconcile to the financing-IC change through the disclosed definition residual. Net cash capex less depreciation is cross-checked against book PPE movement, retaining disposal/book and vintage differences. FY26 PPE reconciles exactly:36.3847 +25.3476 −9.8921 −0.8224 disposal NBV +0.0039 prior excess-DA correction =51.0217 crore; sale proceeds1.0988 less gain0.2764 equal disposal NBV0.8224. Growth ≈ reinvestment × incremental return is a warning check, not a guaranteed identity: fast revenue growth requires capital unless turns or margins improve. Maintenance is undisclosed. Base replacement cash5.5% of sales exceeds book DA4.84%;4.84% and6.5% are tested. Growth productive assets require25–28% of incremental sales in the scenarios. No claim that all capex is discretionary growth is made.\\n\\n\"","\"Management, governance and execution\"","\"Audit chronology matters more than the opinion headline:\\n1. FY21 and FY22: A.K. Lunawath, unmodified financial and effective-IFC opinions; no reported fraud/default. FY22 explicitly reports no auditor resignation that year.\\n2. FY23: Gupta Agarwal successor report records Iyer & Co resignation during that year. Exact intervening appointment, reason and handover documentation remain ND. The later Gupta/Jay Gupta name change is not another resignation. A small₹3,088 professional-tax arrear was reported.\\n3. FY24: unmodified financial/IFC opinions, but audit trail did not operate throughout all transactions; CARO says no commensurate internal audit; ₹9.51 lakh CSR remained unspent. Balance confirmations were subject to adjustments.\\n4. FY25: audit-trail failure recurs; two employees misappropriated₹85 lakh, detected by management with disciplinary/legal action. Recovery, insurance and charge/receivable treatment have not been reconciled. It is not described as promoter theft.\\n5. FY26: audit trail and internal audit reported functioning, no new fraud reported, but secretarial audit finds missing post-listing shareholder approval for material SLB transactions, MD remuneration beyond the specifically approved limit, and a delayed MGT-14. Those findings are not erased by unmodified financial and IFC opinions. [E02,E13]\\n\\nFY26 related-party sales were approximately₹21.529 crore and related-party trade receivables₹7.0585 crore. The report's comparative tables labelled FY25 reproduce FY24 values. Original FY25 SLB sales₹18.674 crore and receivable₹3.7816 crore cannot be replaced by the older labelled figures. A corrected note and counterparty ledger are prerequisites, not evidence of diversion by themselves. FY25 also disclosed an₹8 crore SLB loan and₹0.615 crore interest; mapping movements to closing borrowing categories is required. The deceased-director₹0.1651 crore write-off is distinct from the₹0.85 crore employee event. [E03]\\n\\nFY26 MD/executive remuneration₹0.30/₹0.24 crore is modest relative to PAT, but authorization matters independently of size. September2026 voting passed the remuneration and prospective₹25 crore each SLB/Sri Kamakshi RPT proposals, with promoter abstention on RPT resolutions. This is a positive procedural development, not retrospective proof that FY26 was compliant. Promoters waived their FY26 dividend; public cash distribution is₹0.289596 crore. [E12]\\n\\nIPO gross proceeds are2,284,800 ×₹192 =₹43.86816 crore, not the₹60.14 crore claimed in annual-report narrative. Intended vehicle, working-capital and general-corporate use must be reconciled with actual capex, issue expenses and FD balances. FY25 cash issue expenses₹6.9879 crore and FY26₹0.0246 crore are retained; they are not recurring EBITDA costs. A current complete utilization statement and earmark status are still needed.\\n\\nPromises versus delivery: the prospectus fleet base and subsequently reported additions show investment; FY26 management reports72,689 trips/2.01 crore km, and Q1 a127-tonne ODC delivery. These operational milestones are positives. The order claim₹230 crore has up-to-one-year tenure but no opening/intake/execution/cancellation reconciliation, customer-level margin or minimum-volume guarantee. It is neither guaranteed incremental revenue nor an extra scoring bonus.\\n\\n\"","\"Financial forensics, cash and funding\"","\"| Window | CFO reported | PAT | Conversion | CFO less FY25 investment gains | Adjusted conversion |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2y | 8.73 | 22.75 | 0.384 | 6.12 | 0.269 |\\n| 3y | 15.19 | 30.82 | 0.493 | 12.58 | 0.408 |\\n| 5y | 47.01 | 38.93 | 1.207 | 44.40 | 1.140 |\\n\\nFY26 receivables rose₹26.3168 crore, much faster than sales; ending debtor days rose from about72 to107. Average days and ending days are shown separately. Billed balances older than six months rose from₹1.1759 crore to₹4.9797 crore. Unbilled₹17.0777 crore represents roughly28% of total receivables. The AS9 KAM addresses evidence of rendered services and later invoicing, not guaranteed collection. [E07]\\n\\nFY26 CFO bridge,₹crore: pre-working-capital cash27.4116; advances release4.1091; receivables absorption26.3168; short provisions1.4752; other assets absorption0.5868; payables2.8200; other liabilities3.6770; cash generated12.5894 less tax4.7778 = CFO7.8116. Statutory liabilities and a capital-sale advance are not customer deposits. Cash capex25.3476 less disposal receipts1.0988, deposits/margin movements and interest produce CFI−23.8290; CFF−9.3500 gives cash decline25.3674 to14.7091, within source rounding. Working capital is not deducted again from CFO.\\n\\nFY25 printed CFO includes investment gains not removed in the reconciliation. Analytical reclassification moves₹2.6095 crore from CFO to CFI: CFO−₹1.6891 crore and CFI−₹9.6233 crore, with total cash unchanged. This is a classification sensitivity, not a restated audit opinion or verified sale-proceeds ledger. FY24 original CFO₹6.6035 crore, prospectus₹6.4594 crore and AR25 comparative₹6.4661 crore are retained; security-deposit, disposal and cash/FD classifications differ. FY21 CFO also includes current-debt movement inside other liabilities; analytical removal₹1.3294004 crore prevents calling it all customer cash. Five-year cash conversion does not excuse the weaker recent three-year record.\\n\\nMarch2026 debt₹17.3994 crore consists of vehicles₹12.9101 crore and OD₹4.4893 crore;₹7.4734 crore vehicle maturities are current. Cash equivalents₹14.7091 crore include liquid FD₹12.2691 crore; margin FD₹0.4937 crore is excluded. Available IPO-earmarked liquidity is not independently established, so exclusion sensitivities are explicit. Government balances are not presumed instantly refundable. Opening tax/statutory settlement is stressed separately from future income tax, without double-counting operating WC.\\n\\nThe primary Q1 announcement confirms some bank financing but no amount, interest, term, security or covenant schedule. Therefore October net cash is not known. Model pre-entry financing amounts0,4,8.645 and12.35 crore are assumptions, not disclosures. Existing vehicle amortization is allocated from audited current maturities; remaining debt is conservatively allocated over two years. Q1 financing is tested as48-month debt at10.5%, not asserted contractual terms. OD renewal and additional headroom are explicitly uncommitted. No bank-rating search result is interpreted as proof that the company is unrated.\\n\\n\"","\"Valuation and market expectations\"","\"₹208.50 × 7,434,800 shares = ₹155.01558 crore. FY26 reported P/E 12.43x; normalized P/E 13.63x; P/B 1.90x. March-anchored EV ₹157.71 crore and EV/operating EBITDA 5.86x are stale-balance diagnostics, not current EV. Mean/median60-session traded value ₹1904736.00/₹1332600.00. Reference ₹5 lakh exit at5% participation 7.504 sessions; at2% 18.760 and10% 3.752. One600-sharelot₹125,100;three₹375,300;four₹500,400.\\n\\nForty-five issuer rows occur across60 exchange sessions;15 absent rows are treated as zero capacity, not falsely certified zero trades. All latest20 sessions have issuer rows. Execution assumes participation, not guaranteed fills. The static master price range and market-maker fields are not observed bid–ask spreads. L starts5 under the prescribed schedule and receives an explicit one-point implementation haircut for missing tape days and lot granularity.\\n\\n### Linked valuation design\\nThe model begins31 March2026, bridges202 days to19 October, then runs60 monthly periods and a12-month terminal continuation. Q1 revenue/capex are primary management facts; monthly allocation and all remaining activity, margin, turnover and timing inputs are assumptions. Four intramonth cycles pay80% of hired-trip costs before collection. Gross collections less gross operating payments exactly reconcile to EBITDA less ΔONWC. Capex, principal, interest and tax are actual model cash events, not year-end plugs. One/two/eight-cycle timing sensitivities are retained.\\n\\nOptional rescue: up to₹5.5107 crore analyst-sized bank headroom at12%, and up to₹15 crore gross equity at₹156.375,25% below entry, with3% fee. Equity uses ceiling whole-share issuance and is additionally bounded by10 million authorized shares. No pre-entry equity is invented, because current issued shares are verified. No rescue is injected after the first unfunded breach. Bank renewal is not equivalent to a committed facility. Cash breaches and financing triggers use the same 1e-24 crore numerical tolerance, so Decimal arithmetic residue alone never causes a bank draw or one-share equity issue; this is not an economic funding allowance.\\n\\nThe illustrative conditional bridge assumes₹8.645 crore Q1 bank finance and pre-entry DSO85; neither is verified. It is compared to the no-quantified-draw bridge, not adopted as the actual opening balance sheet. A lower entry price does not cure company funding failure, and a lower future issue price creates more dilution.\\n\\n| Case | First breach | New shares | Terminal cash required / available | Conditional terminal price | IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Unresolved-entry bear no_new_finance | 2026-05-31 opening_statutory_payable | 0 | 4.54 / 3.00 | NS | NS |\\n| Conditional-bank/collections bear no_new_finance | 2027-03-19 OD_due_no_assumed_renewal | 0 | 4.54 / 1.52 | NS | NS |\\n| Unresolved-entry bear bounded_optional | 2026-05-31 cycle_1_80pct_hire_pre_payment | 0 | 14.54 / 8.81 | NS | NS |\\n| Conditional-bank/collections bear bounded_optional | None | 591106 | 14.54 / 16.74 | 49.60 | -24.96% |\\n| Unresolved-entry base no_new_finance | 2026-05-31 opening_statutory_payable | 0 | 7.10 / 6.25 | NS | NS |\\n| Conditional-bank/collections base no_new_finance | 2027-03-19 cycle_1_80pct_hire_pre_payment | 0 | 7.10 / 4.76 | NS | NS |\\n| Unresolved-entry base bounded_optional | 2026-06-30 existing_vehicle_principal | 0 | 17.10 / 12.06 | NS | NS |\\n| Conditional-bank/collections base bounded_optional | None | 409475 | 17.10 / 17.51 | 201.78 | -0.65% |\\n| Unresolved-entry bull no_new_finance | 2026-05-31 opening_statutory_payable | 0 | 9.80 / 28.64 | NS | NS |\\n| Conditional-bank/collections bull no_new_finance | 2027-11-19 cycle_1_80pct_hire_pre_payment | 0 | 9.80 / 27.15 | NS | NS |\\n| Unresolved-entry bull bounded_optional | 2026-06-30 existing_vehicle_principal | 0 | 19.80 / 34.44 | NS | NS |\\n| Conditional-bank/collections bull bounded_optional | None | 0 | 18.84 / 33.51 | 482.33 | 18.26% |\\n\\n### Conditional bear annual bridge\\n| Year | Sales | EBITDA | NOPAT | CFO | Capex | ΔONWC | FCFF | Interest | Principal | End cash | End debt | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | 205.00 | 20.50 | 7.93 | 2.41 | 11.27 | 15.95 | -12.37 | 2.08 | 7.08 | 3.32 | 19.61 | 7939206 |\\n| 2 | 205.00 | 20.50 | 7.93 | 18.31 | 11.27 | 0.00 | 6.58 | 1.84 | 4.88 | 4.96 | 14.73 | 8025906 |\\n| 3 | 210.00 | 22.05 | 8.91 | 18.20 | 11.55 | 1.22 | 6.31 | 1.39 | 3.29 | 6.93 | 11.44 | 8025906 |\\n| 4 | 215.00 | 22.58 | 9.13 | 18.60 | 11.83 | 1.22 | 6.48 | 1.14 | 1.44 | 11.11 | 10.00 | 8025906 |\\n| 5 | 220.00 | 23.10 | 9.34 | 19.04 | 12.31 | 1.22 | 6.45 | 1.09 | 0.00 | 16.74 | 10.00 | 8025906 |\\n\\n### Conditional base annual bridge\\n| Year | Sales | EBITDA | NOPAT | CFO | Capex | ΔONWC | FCFF | Interest | Principal | End cash | End debt | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | 240.00 | 31.68 | 15.05 | 15.85 | 16.61 | 11.25 | -2.23 | 1.75 | 7.08 | 4.80 | 16.05 | 7434800 |\\n| 2 | 275.00 | 37.13 | 17.86 | 25.33 | 24.58 | 6.28 | 0.25 | 1.79 | 4.88 | 5.95 | 14.73 | 7670569 |\\n| 3 | 310.00 | 42.78 | 20.83 | 29.90 | 26.50 | 6.28 | 2.98 | 1.39 | 3.29 | 6.69 | 11.44 | 7809014 |\\n| 4 | 345.00 | 48.30 | 23.70 | 34.40 | 28.43 | 6.28 | 5.61 | 1.14 | 1.44 | 10.54 | 10.00 | 7844275 |\\n| 5 | 380.00 | 53.20 | 26.11 | 38.49 | 30.35 | 6.28 | 7.79 | 1.09 | 0.00 | 17.51 | 10.00 | 7844275 |\\n\\n### Conditional bull annual bridge\\n| Year | Sales | EBITDA | NOPAT | CFO | Capex | ΔONWC | FCFF | Interest | Principal | End cash | End debt | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | 270.00 | 37.80 | 18.55 | 26.71 | 20.96 | 5.34 | 5.24 | 1.71 | 7.08 | 11.50 | 14.10 | 7434800 |\\n| 2 | 330.00 | 49.50 | 25.15 | 33.41 | 33.15 | 8.00 | -0.16 | 1.21 | 4.88 | 7.19 | 10.86 | 7434800 |\\n| 3 | 400.00 | 64.00 | 33.48 | 43.80 | 39.50 | 9.34 | 3.86 | 1.20 | 3.29 | 9.29 | 10.01 | 7434800 |\\n| 4 | 470.00 | 77.55 | 41.10 | 54.77 | 43.35 | 9.34 | 11.01 | 1.03 | 1.44 | 18.56 | 9.04 | 7434800 |\\n| 5 | 550.00 | 93.50 | 50.16 | 66.35 | 50.25 | 10.67 | 15.69 | 0.97 | 0.00 | 33.51 | 9.04 | 7434800 |\\n\\nConditional return attribution (price factor = normalized-profit factor × share-count factor × implied P/E factor):\\n| Case | Profit factor | Share factor | Implied P/E factor | Price factor |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear | 0.75 | 0.93 | 0.34 | 0.24 |\\n| base | 2.22 | 0.95 | 0.46 | 0.97 |\\n| bull | 4.35 | 1.00 | 0.53 | 2.31 |\\n\\nProductive fixed capital follows an economic target, not an asserted GAAP NBV forecast. Cash maintenance above book DA is an explicit replacement/retirement gap: opening productive assets + capex − DA − gap = closing productive assets. The gap is retained monthly and annually. It represents replacement cost beyond historical book depreciation, not free new capacity.\\n\\nEarned terminal EV = next-year FCFF / (discount rate − growth), using operating NOPAT, working-capital growth, productive-asset/deposit growth, replacement above DA and growing retained cash buffer. Base discount15%, growth3%; discount13/17% and growth0/4% tested. Terminal debt is settled once, and a fixed-point buffer funds12 months of operating cycles and reinvestment without new finance. Positive recurring FCFF and recurring cash are required. A loss-making continuation with enough cash for one year cannot earn an ordinary going-concern return. EV plus cash less debt and retained buffer is divided by diluted shares.4/6/8x EBITDA cross-checks are assumptions, not claimed peer multiples. The DCF diagnostic is not a shareholder NPV if future outside equity finances growth.\\n\\n| Test | Status | Target price | Margin bracket | Feasible high price |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 12pct | cash-feasible price root | 367.45 | 15.00%–15.00% | 367.45 |\\n| 15pct | cash-feasible price root | 419.37 | 15.74%–15.74% | 419.37 |\\n| 18pct | cash-feasible price root | 477.00 | 16.59%–16.59% | 477.00 |\\n| fivefold | cash-feasible price root | 1042.50 | 24.90%–24.90% | 1042.50 |\\n| tenfold | no feasible root within6–35% margin bound | 2085.00 | 6.00%–35.00% | NS |\\n\\nA price root is labelled only when both endpoint paths survive and their price residuals bracket zero. Otherwise the result is a joint feasibility threshold, possibly driven by cash or dilution discontinuity. Every root starts with a bounded scan before bisection; failed cash tests and all trials remain in the canonical record.12/15/18% entry limits are conditional algebraic hurdle values, not buy prices. Two-way margin/DSO, discount/replacement, growth, issue-price, bank-headroom, earmark and timing sensitivities are available. No probability weighting is applied to unsupported financing paths.\\n\\n\"","\"Bear case and permanent-loss risks\"","\"The bear case combines lower margin, slower collections, receivable cash loss and replacement investment. It can require dilution even if eventual accounting profit remains positive. Capital can be permanently impaired through uncollectible unbilled/RPT balances, loss of a large customer, stranded specialized equipment or expensive rescue issuance. This is different from temporary share-price volatility.\\n\\nCollateral sale values, recovery priority, accident insurance and lender enforcement costs are insufficient for a credible liquidation floor. Book PPE is not cash at par. Historical accident/collection proceedings in the prospectus are not proven closed by a later general audit statement that no litigation affects financial position. No criminal conviction or promoter fraud is inferred. [E14]\\n\\n\"","\"Fivefold and tenfold feasibility\"","\"₹208.50 requires terminal₹1,042.50 for5x and₹2,085 for10x before shareholder dividends. These imply roughly38.0% and58.5% five-year price CAGRs, not ordinary bull outcomes. The reverse solver tests those prices with the same pre-entry cash, hire prepayments, growth capital, loan repayment, bounded whole-share dilution and earned terminal reinvestment. A missing feasible root is retained as failure, not extrapolated beyond the35% margin bound.\\n\\nBottom-up throughput equivalents are retained for each year: FY26 gross realization and reported2.01 crore km/72,689 trips translate scenario sales into equivalent km/trips; a300-vehicle equivalent at Q1 annualized sales is also shown. These are not physical capacity forecasts because reported trips include hired vehicles, and own/hired utilization is ND. The bull reaches₹550 crore sales, demanding much larger accessible customer volumes, working capital and productive assets. No unverifiable national-market share is used as permission to assume that scale.\\n\\n\"","\"Monitoring dashboard\"","\"1. Obtain corrected FY26 RPT comparative notes and reconcile SLB/Sri Kamakshi balances with original FY25 ledgers.\\n2. Obtain the October cash/debt bridge, Q1 vehicle loan sanctions, repayment schedules and actual draw amounts before assigning BAL/R.\\n3. Track unbilled-to-billed conversion and cash collection; investigate ending AR days above100 and older-than-six-month balances above₹5 crore.\\n4. Reconcile the₹85 lakh employee event: recognized loss, recovery, insurance and documented control remediation.\\n5. Verify audit-trail coverage, independent internal audit and timely RPT/remuneration approvals; another recurrence weakens G1.\\n6. Require current fleet count, additions/disposals, age, owned/hired revenue and replacement budget; reconcile claimed fleet NBV to audited PPE.\\n7. Obtain IPO utilization and FD-earmark schedules; do not count restricted funds as distributable liquidity.\\n8. Reconcile orders to remaining executable revenue, margins, advances and cancellation rights; track recognized sales separately.\\n9. Seek sustained CFO/PAT above0.8 with no classification boost or supplier stretching before lifting CASH3.\\n10. Refresh60-session liquidity, lot size and actual exchange restrictions before any implementation decision.\\n\\n\"","\"Final classification and conditional action\"","\"**Avoid — governance, with a separate unresolved-data Hold.** Governance cap and missing organic-growth/current-funding inputs prevent a defensible composite investment score. This is not a claim that the business is worthless or insolvent. It is a refusal to treat a conditional upside model as a financeable shareholder outcome.\\n\\nReconsider only after corrected disclosures, bank/collection evidence and durable operating cash conversion. Recompute the whole model rather than changing only the P/E. No unconditional buy range, position size or guaranteed liquidity is offered. Scenario limits are decision diagnostics, not personalized financial advice.\\n\\n\"","\"Sources, assumptions and open questions\"","\"| ID | Source / date | Reviewed scope |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| AR26 | [FY2025–26 annual report](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31280_RAPIDFLEET_2025_2026_A_19045203_03092026184433.pdf) 2026-09-03 | PDF123–134 complete financial audit/CARO/IFC;135–162 accounts and notes;106–113 complete secretarial report/annexure;41–72 AGM notice;76–103 directors and MD&A |\\n| AR25 | [FY2024–25 annual report](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_28974_RAPIDFLEET_2024_2025_A_6055495_08092025213555.pdf) 2025-09-08 | PDF90–104 complete financial audit/CARO/IFC;105–135 accounts/notes and original RPT; directors report and IPO narrative |\\n| AR24 | [Original FY2023–24 annual report](https://rapidfleet.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Annual-Report_23-24_compressed-1.pdf) 2024-08-08 | PDF9–18 complete statutory audit/IFC/CARO;19–40 statements/notes. Audit trail, internal audit and CSR adverse observations explicitly retained. |\\n| AR23 | [Original FY2022–23 annual report and filed accounts](https://rapidfleet.in/wp-content/uploads/pdfs/AnnualReport/Annual%20Report_22-23-min.pdf) 2023-09-30 | PDF29–38 complete financial audit/IFC/CARO; financials and notes, original-versus-restated bridge |\\n| AR22 | [Original FY2021–22 annual report](https://rapidfleet.in/wp-content/uploads/pdfs/AnnualReport/Annual%20Report_21-22-min.pdf) 2022-09-05 | PDF9–21 complete statutory audit/CARO/IFC;22–35 financial statements and notes;restatement differences preserved. |\\n| AR21 | [Original FY2020–21 annual report](https://rapidfleet.in/wp-content/uploads/pdfs/AnnualReport/Annual%20Report_20-21-min.pdf) 2021-11-30 | PDF10–19 complete statutory audit/CARO/IFC;20–32 financials and notes. Revenue/DA/capex visually checked;current debt in OCL separately identified. |\\n| PROSPECTUS | [Final IPO prospectus](https://rapidfleet.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Rapid-Fleet_Prospectu.pdf) 2025-03-26 | PDF risk factors25–50;IPOobjects and sharecapital;business123–146;restatementaudit211–214;financials215–237;litigation. Restatement report not treated as original annual audit. |\\n| FY26UPDATE | [FY26 performance and fleet update](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPIDFLEET_22052026093226_pr220526__1___1_.pdf) 2026-05-22 | All5pages;management measures distinguished from audited operating EBITDA. Company August investor-corner copy byte-identical, not a new update. |\\n| Q127 | [Q1FY27 business update](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPIDFLEET_31082026085830_business_merged.pdf) 2026-08-31 | All6pages;Q1revenue53.60vs32.93, capex12.35, bank financing amount ND, vehicle and orders;not audited quarter P&L or balance sheet |\\n| DIV | [Promoter dividend waiver](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPIDFLEET_23092026194401_RFMSLBoardMeetingOutcome23092026Signed.pdf) 2026-09-23 | Both pages: irrevocable FY26 promoter waiver;public entitlement unchanged;18Sep record date |\\n| AGM | [AGM proceedings](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPIDFLEET_25092026204100_RFMSLAGMProceedings25092026FinalSigned.pdf) 2026-09-25 | All5pages;agenda/waiver, no speaker requests;voting confirmation separately sourced |\\n| VOTE | [AGM voting and scrutinizer report](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPIDFLEET_28092026175031_SCRUTINIZERR.pdf) 2026-09-28 | All8 resolutions;RPTpromoter5,021,500 abstentions;prospectiveFY27 caps passed |\\n| PEER_TCI | [Transport Corporation of India FY25 annual report](https://tcil.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Annual-Report-2024-25.pdf) FY2025, available before cutoff | Business model and segment scope only, not a full peer financial audit |\\n| PEER_VRL | [VRL Logistics FY25 annual report](https://www.vrlgroup.in/investor_download/Annual_Report_2024_25.pdf) FY2025, available before cutoff | Business model and segment scope only, not a full peer financial audit |\\n| PEER_RITCO | [Ritco Logistics FY25 annual report](https://www.ritcologistics.com/frontassets/images/Annual_Report_2024-2025.pdf) FY2025, available before cutoff | Issuer presentation business scope: bulk/FTL, owned and aggregated vehicles, warehousing; no contemporary fleet count or peer multiple inferred. FY25 annual report indexed scope cross-check only. |\\n\\nDated NSE security-master and all60 issuer-specific bhavcopy extracts, public URLs and original-byte hashes are in the source-safe inputs. The framework filename, version, byte count and hash are retained. No source PDF, page image, raw extraction dump, transfer helper or private storage identifier is included.\\n\\nThe canonical dataset retains 213 deduplicated full paths and all calculation rows, including failed roots,67 monthly/pre-entry records per path, intraperiod events and terminal events. Minimal authored inputs and standard-library code suffice for offline reconstruction. Verification checks arithmetic, hashes, event recurrence, bounded finance, score caps/NS, path references, cash survival and terminal continuing capital. A clean recovery deletes generated outputs and old attestations before rebuilding under a read/network guard; verification output is regenerated, not trusted from a copied badge.\\n\\nOpen items are substantive: exact organic demand, current customer concentration, maintenance capex, asset unit economics, original-to-restated account reconciliations, employee-loss accounting, historical auditor-transition reasons, current bank terms, IPO earmarks and legal-case status. None is silently filled with management optimism.\\n\"","\"heading_start\"","\"body_start\"","\"end\"","\"original_heading\"","\"## 1. Executive verdict\\n\"","3197","\"## 2. Research header, gates and data confidence\\n\"","3235","8850","\"## 3. Scorecard and calculation audit\\n\"","10393","\"## 4. Business overview and economic engine\\n\"","10439","12554","\"## 5. Moat, industry and competitive position\\n\"","12585","15230","\"## 6. Revenue growth and scale\\n\"","